 "observations": [
  "The page number «193» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 78», in bold sans serif, underlined. At the foot, the signature line of the printed sheet: «D. K. D. Vidensk. Selsk. Skr., 7. Række, hist. og filos. Afd. III. 1.» on the left and the sheet number «25» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line. The title (L01), «Eclipsis solis spatio longinquo — Eclipsis solis spatio propinquo», the solar eclipse at the far and at the near distance, in bold, without sigla. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of the title in two lines, «C 86v, O 143r,» and «N 49r—49v.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: sixteen columns in two halves divided by double vertical rules, the far distance at the left and the near at the right, each with «Argumentum latitudinis» over «Gr.», «Min.», «Gr.», «Min.», «Digiti defectus» over «Dig.», «Min.», and «Dakaicae1) casus» over «Min.», «Sec.», note 2 at the first «casus» and note 3 at the first «Min.»: see the critical note. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L02-L04); the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. The left half has twenty-nine rows (L05-L33), the right thirty-one (L05-L35); the rows of the left half are empty in L34-L35. Specks after the 0 of 173° 0′ (L06) and over the 181 of 181° 30′ (L23) in the right half; not transcribed.",
  "L05-L35. The arguments of latitude every half degree on either side of the node, 0° 30′ to 6° 30′ and 359° 30′ to 353° 30′, with their supplements, between the limits of an eclipse: at the far distance 6° 37′ (173° 23′, L05) and, as the last row, 353° 0′ (187° 0′, L33), seven degrees from the node; at the near distance 7° 11′ (172° 49′, L05) and 352° 30′ (187° 30′, L35), seven and a half degrees from it, C's readings, where O and N have 352° 49′ and N 187° 11′ (notes k and m), the limit 7° 11′ on the other side of the node. The values of each half are symmetric about the node in every row: the same digits and casus stand at the same distance on either side. Suter's limits of the solar eclipse, 172° 49′ to 187° 30′, 0° to 7° 11′ and 352° 30′ to 360° (p. 94, JP2 0126, L16-L20), are the first and the last row of the right half.",
  "L05-L35, continued. Checked, with the radius of the sun 16′ 17″ at its mean distance (Tab. 63, p. 177, JP2 0209) and of the moon 14′ 38″ and 17′ 17″ at its greatest and least distance (Tab. 61 and 66, pp. 175 and 180, JP2 0207 and 0212): the casus at the node, 30′ 55″ and 33′ 34″ (L19, L20), are the sums of the two radii, and the digits at the node, 10 dig. 48′ and 12 dig. 44′, the diameter of the moon in twelfths of that of the sun, 10 dig. 47.4′ and 12 dig. 44.2′. In the other rows, with the latitude 4° 30′ · sin x and the sums S of the radii, the digits, (S − β) · 12 : 2 · 16′ 17″, agree within 3.3′ in every row except near the limits and at 4° 30′ and 5° 0′ of the near half; the casus, √(S² − β²), within 10″ from 0° to 5° except at 4° 0′ of the near half (27′ 13″ for 27′ 47″, −34″), and are larger towards the limits, by 15″ to 51″ at 5° 30′-6° 30′ (far) and 14″ to 78″ at 5° 30′-7° 0′ (near): the table answers there to sums of about 31′ 1″ and 33′ 41″, and its limits, 6° 37′ and 7° 11′, lie 2½′ beyond the arguments at which β reaches 30′ 55″ and 33′ 34″, 6° 34½′ and 7° 8½′.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked by the symmetry and the computation: the edition's readings agree with the symmetric row and the computation at 6° 37′ and 173° 23′ (notes b and f: the 37 and 23 of O and N against C's 38 and 33), at 355° 0′ in the digits of the far half (note h: 2 dig. 45′ against the 55 of C and O; 2 dig. 43.3′ computed), at 358° 0′ in the casus (note i: N's 29′ 28″ against C's 18 and O's 38; 29′ 26.7″ computed), at 4° 0′ and 356° 0′ in the digits of the near half (notes o and p: C's 5 dig. 28′ against O's 38, O's 5 against C's 6), at 0° 0′ and 354° 0′ in the casus (notes r and s: C's 33′ 34″, omitted in O, and C's 18′ 32″ against O's 31). At 2° 30′ (note g) O's 7 dig. 6′, the edition's reading, and C's 7 dig. 7′ lie 2.9′ and 3.9′ above the computation, 7 dig. 3.1′; the symmetric row, 357° 30′, has 7 dig. 6′. At 4° 30′ (note n) the edition has O's 4 dig. 27′, against C's 37, as at 355° 30′ (L29), where no note is given: C's 4 dig. 37′ is the nearer to the computation, 4 dig. 33.8′, and keeps the steps of the column, 51′ and 50′ from 5 dig. 28′ to 3 dig. 47′, where 27 gives 61′ and 40′. At 355° 0′ (note q) the edition's 3 dig. 47′, for the 45 of C and O, is the value of the symmetric row, 5° 0′ (L10); the 45 of the manuscripts is the nearer to the computation, 3 dig. 41.9′. At 353° 0′ (note t) the edition's 7′ 56″, for the 51 of C and O, is the value of the symmetric row, 7° 0′ (L06); the computation gives 6′ 38″. The rows of the degrees 355, 355 and 354 at 355° 30′-354° 30′ (notes a-a, «a-a)» at 355° 30′ and at 354° 30′): O has 357. Notes c, d, e, k, l and m concern the arguments: C's 0° 30′ and 0° 0′ at the node of the far half, omitted in O; O's 187 at the last row of the far half, against C's 186; C's 172° 30′ at the first row of the near half (note l), against O's 49.",
  "Notes 1-2: N's «Minuta» for «Dakaicae»; «elzukut» (C) and «elzukuz» (O), al-suqūṭ, for «casus». Italics: «om.». N02: «355. . . .354», the four points of the lemma as printed."
